    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d31">

                <pb facs="0189" n="75" xml:id="pg75"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">311.3 B 1/229</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berne</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-10-14"><hi rendition="#italic">October 14,
                        1924</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received October 28.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 170</seg>
                    <lb/>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">L.N. No. 597</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to my despatch
                    No. 153, of October 2, 1924 (L.N. No. 593),<anchor n="49" xml:id="d31-f1"/>
                    enclosing a document (No. A.115.1924.IX) issued by the Secretariat of the League
                    of Nations and embodying a resolution adopted by the Assembly and Council,
                    requesting the interested governments to inform the Secretary General, before
                    the Council meets in December 1924, whether they are prepared to take part in a
                    conference to be convened in April or May 1925 for the purpose of discussing the
                    Draft Convention for the Control of the International Traffic in Arms, Munitions
                    and Implements of War.</p>
                <p>I have now received a letter, dated October 9, 1924, from the Secretary General
                    of the League, enclosing a communication (C.L.140.1924.IX) addressed to the
                    Secretary of State in the sense of the above mentioned resolution. This
                    communication, as well as a copy of Sir Eric Drummond’s letter to me, is
                    transmitted herewith.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hugh Gibson</hi></signed></closer>




                <xi:include href="d31-a1.xml" xpointer="d31-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d31-a2.xml" xpointer="d31-a2"/>
